::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which presentation claim is clearest for school leaders? {
=A. The school should provide a supervised after-school study space for students who remain on campus. #Correct. This claim states a clear proposal for a specific audience.
~B. School has some problems. #Incorrect. This is too vague.
~C. People need stuff after school. #Incorrect. This is informal and unclear.
~D. My slides are about many things. #Incorrect. This lacks focus.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which evidence best supports the claim? {
~A. Students probably need somewhere to go. #Incorrect. This is a guess.
=B. In a survey of 120 students, 68 reported staying on campus after dismissal at least twice a week. #Correct. This is specific and credible survey evidence.
~C. The presenter likes the idea. #Incorrect. This is personal opinion.
~D. A study space sounds nice. #Incorrect. This is not evidence.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide title best presents a finding? {
~A. Things We Found #Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. Our Presentation #Incorrect. This does not state a finding.
=C. Survey Evidence Shows Many Students Stay After School #Correct. This title identifies the source and finding.
~D. Random Data #Incorrect. This makes the evidence sound unorganized.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which speaker note best explains evidence? {
~A. This number is here because it is important. #Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. The slide already says it. #Incorrect. The speaker should explain the evidence.
~C. I found a number in the survey. #Incorrect. This identifies source but not meaning.
=D. Because more than half of surveyed students remain after school, the proposal addresses a need that affects many students. #Correct. This explains what the evidence means and connects it to the claim.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which sentence uses the best tone? {
=A. The proposal would provide students with a supervised academic workspace after dismissal. #Correct. This is formal and precise.
~B. This would be a cool place for kids. #Incorrect. This is casual.
~C. People should just make it happen. #Incorrect. This is demanding and not academic.
~D. The thing is good. #Incorrect. This is vague.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is grammatically correct? {
~A. Students needs a supervised study space. #Incorrect. The plural subject needs "need."
=B. Students need a supervised study space. #Correct. The subject and verb agree.
~C. Because students need a supervised study space. #Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~D. Students need, a supervised study space. #Incorrect. The comma is unnecessary.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best fits the audience of school leaders? {
~A. Kids hang around and need a place. #Incorrect. This is too informal.
~B. This thing would help people. #Incorrect. This is vague.
=C. Students who remain after dismissal need a supervised place to complete academic work. #Correct. This is precise, formal, and audience-aware.
~D. Everyone knows this should happen. #Incorrect. This is unsupported.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which visual design choice best supports the evidence? {
~A. Tiny text with no label #Incorrect. The audience may not understand the data.
~B. A decorative image covering the chart #Incorrect. This hides evidence.
~C. Multiple unrelated animations #Incorrect. These distract from the message.
=D. A readable chart with a clear title, labels, and source context #Correct. This helps the audience understand the evidence.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which final review step is most important before submitting? {
=A. Check that the claim, evidence, slide, speaker note, tone, and conventions align. #Correct. This confirms the presentation is ready to share.
~B. Check only the background color. #Incorrect. Readiness requires more than design.
~C. Delete the speaker notes. #Incorrect. The oral component matters.
~D. Ignore feedback. #Incorrect. Feedback should guide revision.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] What is the purpose of an oral explanation? {
~A. To repeat every slide word exactly #Incorrect. Oral explanation should add reasoning.
=B. To help the audience understand how evidence supports the message #Correct. The speaker interprets evidence and guides understanding.
~C. To avoid discussing evidence #Incorrect. Evidence should be explained.
~D. To make the presentation unrelated to the slide #Incorrect. Oral and digital parts should align.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Why should survey size be included with the result? {
~A. It makes the slide look longer. #Incorrect. Length is not the purpose.
~B. It replaces explanation. #Incorrect. Evidence still needs explanation.
=C. It tells the audience how many people provided the data. #Correct. This helps the audience judge the evidence.
~D. It hides the source. #Incorrect. It gives more source context.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide problem should be fixed first? {
~A. The slide has a clear title. #Incorrect. That is a strength, not a problem.
~B. The slide labels the chart. #Incorrect. Labels help understanding.
~C. The slide uses readable font. #Incorrect. Readability is a strength.
=D. The slide has important numbers in tiny unreadable text. #Correct. Evidence must be readable.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which revision improves formal tone? {
=A. Replace "awesome idea" with "effective proposal." #Correct. This changes casual wording into formal academic wording.
~B. Replace "proposal" with "thing." #Incorrect. This is less precise.
~C. Replace "evidence" with "stuff." #Incorrect. This is informal.
~D. Replace "students" with "kids hanging around." #Incorrect. This is less formal.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence correctly uses a comma after an introductory phrase? {
~A. After dismissal many students, remain on campus. #Incorrect. The comma is misplaced.
=B. After dismissal, many students remain on campus. #Correct. The comma correctly follows the introductory phrase.
~C. After, dismissal many students remain on campus. #Incorrect. This splits the phrase incorrectly.
~D. After dismissal many students remain, on campus. #Incorrect. This comma is unnecessary.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Feedback says the presentation does not explain why evidence matters. What should be revised? {
~A. Only the slide color #Incorrect. Color does not explain evidence.
=B. The speaker note, so it interprets the evidence and connects it to the claim #Correct. This directly addresses the feedback.
~C. The file name only #Incorrect. This does not improve the presentation.
~D. The evidence by deleting it #Incorrect. Evidence should be explained, not removed.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which presentation is publication-ready? {
~A. A presentation with clear slides but unrelated speaker notes #Incorrect. The parts must align.
~B. A presentation with evidence but many grammar errors #Incorrect. Conventions matter.
~C. A presentation with formal notes but unreadable slides #Incorrect. Digital support must be clear.
=D. A presentation with aligned slides, clear evidence, formal notes, correct conventions, and feedback-based revision #Correct. This meets final readiness expectations.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which transition best connects evidence to reasoning? {
=A. This evidence matters because #Correct. This phrase signals that the speaker will explain the importance of the evidence.
~B. Anyway #Incorrect. This is informal and does not show reasoning.
~C. Randomly #Incorrect. This does not create a logical connection.
~D. Whatever #Incorrect. This is informal and dismissive.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Why should slides avoid full paragraphs of speaker text? {
~A. The audience should not see any information. #Incorrect. The audience should see key information.
=B. Full paragraphs can overload the audience and weaken the role of oral explanation. #Correct. Slides should highlight evidence while the speaker explains.
~C. Evidence should always be hidden. #Incorrect. Evidence should be clear.
~D. Paragraphs automatically prove the claim. #Incorrect. Reasoning and evidence prove claims.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which statement correctly uses evidence? {
~A. The proposal is good because I like it. #Incorrect. This is opinion.
~B. Students need help because maybe they do. #Incorrect. This is uncertain.
=C. Since 68 of 120 surveyed students stay after school, the need affects a large portion of respondents. #Correct. This uses specific evidence and explains meaning.
~D. Everyone already knows the answer. #Incorrect. This is unsupported.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is a fragment? {
~A. Students remain after school. #Incorrect. This is complete.
~B. The proposal supports students. #Incorrect. This is complete.
~C. The survey result matters. #Incorrect. This is complete.
=D. Because the survey result matters. #Correct. This dependent clause does not complete the thought.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which response best uses collaborator feedback? {
=A. Feedback said the note was vague, so I revised it to explain how the evidence supports the proposal. #Correct. This applies feedback to a specific communication problem.
~B. Feedback said the note was vague, so I ignored it. #Incorrect. This does not improve the work.
~C. Feedback said the note was vague, so I deleted the evidence. #Incorrect. This weakens the presentation.
~D. Feedback said the note was vague, so I changed an unrelated color. #Incorrect. This does not address the issue.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best improves precision? {
~A. Replace "students" with "people." #Incorrect. This is less precise.
=B. Replace "a place" with "a supervised academic workspace." #Correct. This makes the wording more specific.
~C. Replace "evidence" with "stuff." #Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. Replace "proposal" with "thing." #Incorrect. This is less precise.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which speaker note best fits a closing? {
=A. For these reasons, the proposal would address a documented student need after school. #Correct. This closing reinforces the claim and evidence.
~B. That's all I have. #Incorrect. This does not reinforce the message.
~C. I am done now. #Incorrect. This is not a strong academic closing.
~D. The end, I guess. #Incorrect. This is informal and uncertain.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide and note pairing is strongest? {
~A. Slide: Need. Note: This is important and stuff. #Incorrect. Both parts are vague.
=B. Slide: 68 of 120 surveyed students stay after school. Note: This result shows the need affects many students. #Correct. The slide provides evidence, and the note explains it.
~C. Slide: Random image. Note: Survey evidence matters. #Incorrect. The slide does not support the note.
~D. Slide: Full script. Note: Same full script. #Incorrect. This overloads the slide and repeats instead of explaining.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_UA_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which final statement best describes Unit 6 mastery? {
=A. I can present a clear message using oral explanation, digital support, credible evidence, revision, and formal language. #Correct. This captures the full Unit 6 synthesis.
~B. I can make colorful slides without evidence. #Incorrect. Evidence and explanation are required.
~C. I can read slides without explaining them. #Incorrect. Oral explanation matters.
~D. I can submit without checking conventions. #Incorrect. Final work must be reviewed.
}
